---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import leaderboard from '../../data/llm-leaderboard.json';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Evals · LLM leaderboard | Manuel Oelmaier"
	description="Citeable LLM scores with sources — and human subsets of the same benchmarks, so a percentage stops being an abstract compress of nuance."
	ogTitle="Evals · LLM leaderboard"
>
	<section class="fade-in eval-page eval-page-wide">
		<header class="page-header">
			<p class="eval-kicker">Evals</p>
			<h1>LLM leaderboard</h1>
			<p class="section-subtitle">
				Published model numbers with sources — plus a way to feel what those numbers leave out.
			</p>
			<p class="eval-updated">
				Table updated <time datetime={leaderboard.updated}>{leaderboard.updated}</time>
				· curated from papers and system cards
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="eval-idea" aria-labelledby="eval-idea-heading">
			<h2 id="eval-idea-heading">Why take a subset yourself</h2>
			<p>
				Leaderboard scores <strong>compress</strong> a whole evaluation into one number.
				That helps compare models — and it also throws away difficulty mix, failure modes,
				prompting, and whether “human-level” on paper feels anything like answering the items.
			</p>
			<p>
				The core idea here: sit the same kind of multiple-choice items a model was scored on
				(a <em>seeded subset</em> of the real benchmark), so you can see what a published
				accuracy means — and what it does not.
			</p>
			<p class="eval-help">
				Human runs use answered-only scoring and are not full-benchmark replications.
				<a href="/evals/hellaswag/about/">HellaSwag methodology</a>
			</p>
		</section>

		<div id="leaderboard-root" class="eval-body"></div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
	import { mountLeaderboard } from '../../lib/evals/leaderboard';
	import data from '../../data/llm-leaderboard.json';

	const root = document.getElementById('leaderboard-root');
	if (root) {
		mountLeaderboard(root, data);
	}
</script>
